      "status": "sourced",
      "value": "the mirror normal bisects the Sun and the chosen ground site",
      "source": "Wikipedia, 'Reflect Orbital' (Earendil-1 reoriented 'within seconds to keep its beam on a target'). The site choice, lock windows and slew are the app's TargetedReflector machinery."
    },
    {
      "subjects": [
        "sail_0",
        "sail_1",
        "sail_2",
        "sail_3"
      ],
      "status": "derived",
      "value": "four triangular quadrants of an 18 x 18 m square (2819.3 m2 of film after the hub cut-out, the boom clearances and the scallops); outer-edge sag 0.90 m and boom-edge sag 0.25 m read off the figure; cant 0.00 deg",
      "source": "Reflect Orbital, 'Figure 2: Deployed Configuration' (four triangular quadrants, 18000 mm per side, two crossed booms along the diagonals, a 2439.59 mm central bus; the user's reference qa/waves/20261104/1.png, with the render 2.png), 2026-11-04. Coplanar by default: the scalloped edges are the tension design that keeps each quadrant flat, and any cant splits the ground spot (docs/subsystems/satellites.md, 'Reflect Orbital quad sail')."
    },
    {
